# Team Structure

A Six Sigma project only succeeds if the right people are involved in the right roles.

Teams are not built in advance and then assigned to random projects. Every Six Sigma team is built specifically for the project at hand. The people selected, the roles they fill, and the way the team is managed all directly affect whether the improvement succeeds or fails.

This file explains how Six Sigma teams are structured, who the key roles are, what each person is responsible for, and how the team is managed from start to finish.

---

## Two levels of Six Sigma management

Six Sigma is managed at two levels inside an organization.

The first level is the enterprise level. This is where senior leaders, executives, and a leadership council set the overall direction for Six Sigma across the organization. Their job is not to run daily project work. Their job is to create the conditions that allow projects to succeed.

The responsibilities of an enterprise-level Six Sigma leadership group include:
- Creating a rationale for using Six Sigma and building it into the organization's culture.
- Setting clear objectives to make sure project goals align with business goals.
- Holding teams and the organization accountable for improvements and performance.
- Demanding and reviewing measurements of results.
- Communicating successes and failures honestly across the organization.
- Rewarding teams and individuals for Six Sigma achievements.
- Advocating for resources and funding for improvement projects.

The second level is the team level. This is where the actual project work happens. The rest of this file focuses on this level.

---

## Why teams are built per project

Six Sigma teams cannot be generic.

A team that works well for improving a billing process may not be the right team for improving a manufacturing line. Subject matter experts, process owners, and technical resources must match the specific process and problem being addressed.

This is also why organizations often send existing employees for Six Sigma training rather than simply hiring outside experts. An employee who already understands the process brings far more value than an expert who has to learn the environment from scratch.

Team members who are not familiar with the process or the customer should only be included if they bring a specific skill the project genuinely needs.

---

## Minimum team composition

Every Six Sigma process improvement team needs, at a minimum:

- A Six Sigma leader (usually a Black Belt)
- A process owner
- A process expert
- Someone to manage budgeting and accounting

In some cases, the same person fills two of these roles. For example, the process owner may also be the most knowledgeable expert on the process. In more complex projects, the team may also need technical staff such as IT professionals, programmers, HR representatives, legal staff, or compliance officers.

---

## The three types of team members

Every person on a Six Sigma team belongs to one of three categories.

### 1. Regular team members

Regular team members participate in all team activities and attend all or almost all meetings.

This group includes:
- The project leader (Black Belt)
- The process owner
- The process expert
- Key subject matter experts identified as critical to the project

Regular team members should not be asked to serve on more than one project at a time. Their attention and energy need to be fully focused on the project. Organizational leaders may want to reduce daily work requirements for these individuals so they can give the project the time it needs.

Five regular team members is considered a good average size. Teams that are too large create communication problems, make brainstorming harder to manage, and increase the risk of burnout.

### 2. Ad hoc team members

Ad hoc team members are brought in as needed, not for every meeting.

These are usually subject matter experts or employees who work directly with the process being improved. Pulling them out of their daily roles for every session would harm production. Instead, they are included when their specific knowledge is required.

Ad hoc members may attend a handful of meetings over the life of a project. They can also serve on multiple projects simultaneously because their involvement is limited and focused.

### 3. Resource team members

Resource team members are included only when the project leader decides they are needed for a specific meeting or event.

These are typically members of supporting departments such as:
- Accounting
- Human resources
- Compliance
- Legal
